-- Q1: medium / L2 — Land cost computation including demolition and salvage
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Ravenswood Properties purchases a parcel of land for $450,000, paying $12,000 in closing costs and $35,000 to demolish an old warehouse on the site. Salvage materials from the demolition are sold for $6,000. Ravenswood also pays $8,000 for grading and leveling. What amount should Ravenswood record as the cost of the land?', '["$499,000", "$505,000", "$462,000", "$491,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 360-10-30, land cost includes all expenditures to acquire and prepare it for intended use: purchase price ($450,000), closing costs ($12,000), demolition ($35,000), and grading ($8,000), less salvage proceeds ($6,000), totaling $499,000. Choice B ($505,000) is incorrect because it omits the $6,000 salvage reduction; however, salvage from demolished structures must offset land preparation expenditures.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — Idle asset continues to be depreciated
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Clearwater Manufacturing temporarily idles a production machine with a cost of $160,000, salvage value of $10,000, and a 10-year useful life due to reduced demand. The machine has been depreciated for 3 years using the straight-line method. What depreciation expense should Clearwater recognize for Year 4 while the machine is idle?', '["No depreciation expense", "$11,250 per year", "$15,000 per year", "$7,500 per year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, depreciation of a temporarily idle asset continues because the passage of time still erodes the asset''s utility. Annual straight-line depreciation is ($160,000 − $10,000) / 10 = $15,000. Choice A is incorrect because depreciation ceases only when an asset is classified as held for sale or fully depreciated; however, a temporarily idle asset does not meet either condition.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — DDB-to-straight-line switch effect analysis
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Fieldstone Corp. purchased equipment for $200,000 with a 5-year useful life and no salvage value, using double-declining balance depreciation. After Year 2, the book value is $72,000 with 3 years remaining. The controller considers switching to straight-line for the remaining useful life. What is the most likely effect of this switch on Year 3 depreciation expense compared to continuing DDB?', '["No change because the switch is prohibited under ASC 250", "Decrease by $4,800 because straight-line allocates $24,000 versus $28,800 under DDB", "Increase by $4,800 because straight-line front-loads remaining expense", "Decrease by $14,400 because straight-line uses the original 5-year rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45, a change from DDB to straight-line is a change in accounting estimate effected by a change in method, applied prospectively. Straight-line depreciation for the remaining 3 years is $72,000 / 3 = $24,000, compared to DDB of $72,000 × 40% = $28,800, a $4,800 decrease. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 250 permits this change rather than prohibiting it, treating it prospectively as a change in estimate.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Land improvements vs land distinction
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which of the following distinguishes land improvements from land?', '["Land improvements are recorded at fair value rather than historical cost", "Land improvements are expensed immediately because they are not long-lived", "Land improvements include the cost of clearing and grading the land", "Land improvements have a finite useful life and are depreciated separately"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 360-10, land improvements such as parking lots, fencing, and landscaping are recorded as separate depreciable assets because they have finite useful lives, unlike land itself which is never depreciated. Choice C is incorrect because clearing and grading costs are included in the cost of land, not classified as land improvements, since they prepare the land for its intended use.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Composite depreciation rate calculation
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Brookfield Logistics purchases three delivery vehicles: Vehicle A costs $60,000 with a 5-year life, Vehicle B costs $45,000 with a 3-year life, and Vehicle C costs $30,000 with a 6-year life. All vehicles have zero salvage value. Using the composite method, what is the composite depreciation rate?', '["21.4%", "23.7%", "26.7%", "33.3%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, the composite depreciation method applies a single rate to a group of similar assets. The rate equals total annual depreciation divided by total cost: ($12,000 + $15,000 + $5,000) / $135,000 = 23.7%. Choice D (33.3%) is incorrect because it uses the shortest-lived vehicle''s individual rate rather than computing a cost-weighted composite rate across the entire group.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Donated asset initial recording basis
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Under U.S. GAAP, at what value should a donated fixed asset be recorded by the recipient?', '["Fair value at the date of donation", "Donor''s historical cost", "Donor''s net book value", "Zero, because no cost was incurred"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 845-10-30, a donated fixed asset is recorded at its fair value on the date of receipt, reflecting the asset''s economic value to the entity. Choice B (donor''s historical cost) is incorrect because the recipient has no obligation to carry over the donor''s basis; rather than using historical cost, GAAP measures the contribution at fair value to faithfully represent the asset''s worth.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Involuntary conversion gain/loss analysis
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Ridgeway Manufacturing''s factory equipment with a cost of $320,000 and accumulated depreciation of $210,000 is destroyed by fire on June 15, 2025. The insurance company pays $145,000 in settlement. How does the involuntary conversion most likely affect Ridgeway''s income statement?', '["Loss of $175,000 recognized in operating expenses", "Loss of $35,000 recognized in other income and expense", "Gain of $35,000 recognized in other income and expense", "No gain or loss because insurance proceeds replace the asset"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 610-20, when a fixed asset is involuntarily converted, the entity derecognizes the asset and recognizes the difference between insurance proceeds ($145,000) and carrying amount ($110,000) as a $35,000 gain. Choice A ($175,000 loss) is incorrect because it uses the full original cost rather than the carrying amount — the $210,000 accumulated depreciation reduced the book value to $110,000.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Leasehold improvement depreciation period
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'On January 1, 2025, Pinnacle Retail installs $180,000 of leasehold improvements in a leased storefront. The improvements have an estimated useful life of 12 years, but the remaining lease term is 8 years with no renewal option. Using straight-line depreciation, what is Pinnacle''s annual depreciation expense for the leasehold improvements?', '["$12,000", "$15,000", "$18,000", "$22,500"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 842-20-35, leasehold improvements are depreciated over the shorter of their useful life or the remaining lease term. Since the 8-year lease term is shorter than the 12-year useful life, Pinnacle depreciates over 8 years: $180,000 / 8 = $22,500. Choice B ($15,000) is incorrect because it uses the 12-year useful life rather than the shorter lease term, ignoring that the lessee loses the benefit when the lease expires.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Sale-leaseback gain recognition evaluation under ASC 842
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Westfield Industries sells a manufacturing facility with a carrying amount of $2,800,000 to Apex Realty for $3,500,000, the current fair value, and simultaneously leases it back for 3 years at market rent. The facility has a remaining useful life of 20 years. Westfield retains no repurchase option. Under ASC 842-40, should Westfield recognize the $700,000 gain, and what factors are most important in this evaluation?', '["No, because sale-leaseback transactions require deferral of all gains under ASC 842", "Yes, the full $700,000 gain is recognized because the transfer qualifies as a sale at fair value with no repurchase option", "Only a proportional gain based on the ratio of the leaseback term to the remaining useful life", "Defer the gain and amortize it over the 3-year leaseback term as reduced lease expense"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 842-40-25, if the transfer qualifies as a sale under ASC 606 — control transfers at fair value with no repurchase option — the seller-lessee derecognizes the asset and recognizes the full gain. Choice D is incorrect because it reflects the superseded ASC 840 approach; however, ASC 842 eliminated gain deferral for sale-leaseback transactions completed at fair value without repurchase provisions.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Fully depreciated asset still in use
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Under U.S. GAAP, what is the proper accounting treatment for a fixed asset that is fully depreciated but still in use?', '["The asset and its accumulated depreciation remain on the balance sheet with no further depreciation", "The asset is removed from the balance sheet because it has no remaining value", "Additional depreciation is recognized based on the asset''s current fair value", "The asset is written up to fair value and depreciation restarts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 360-10, a fully depreciated asset that remains in use stays on the balance sheet at cost with fully offsetting accumulated depreciation. No additional depreciation is recorded because the depreciable base has been fully allocated. Choice B is incorrect because derecognition occurs only upon disposal or abandonment, rather than merely because the asset has been fully depreciated.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Weighted-average accumulated expenditures calculation
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Northgate Corp. constructs a building for its own use during 2025. Expenditures are: $600,000 on January 1, $480,000 on April 1, and $360,000 on October 1. Construction is completed on December 31, 2025. What are the weighted-average accumulated expenditures for purposes of capitalizing interest?', '["$1,440,000", "$1,140,000", "$1,050,000", "$960,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 835-20, weighted-average accumulated expenditures are computed by weighting each expenditure by the fraction of the year outstanding: $600,000 × 12/12 + $480,000 × 9/12 + $360,000 × 3/12 = $1,050,000. Choice A ($1,440,000) is incorrect because it uses total expenditures rather than time-weighted amounts, which would overstate the capitalizable interest base.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — Environmental remediation vs ARO classification evaluation
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Kelsey Chemical operates a plant on contaminated land. Federal regulations require cleanup upon closure, expected in 15 years, estimated at $2 million. Separately, an EPA inspection revealed existing soil contamination requiring immediate remediation at $500,000 under current law. The CFO proposes recording both as asset retirement obligations under ASC 410-20. Evaluate whether this treatment is appropriate.', '["Both qualify as AROs because they arise from environmental regulations", "Neither qualifies as an ARO because cleanup costs are always expensed under ASC 450", "Only the $500,000 immediate remediation qualifies as an ARO because it is a present obligation", "Only the $2 million closure cost qualifies as an ARO; the $500,000 is an environmental remediation liability under ASC 410-30"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 410-20, an ARO arises from a legal obligation associated with the retirement of a long-lived asset — the $2 million closure obligation meets this definition. The $500,000 existing contamination relates to current compliance rather than future retirement. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 410-20 requires a direct link to asset retirement rather than general environmental obligations — existing contamination is addressed under ASC 410-30 or ASC 450-20.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — R&D equipment with alternative future use capitalized
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Whitfield Biotech purchases specialized laboratory equipment for $275,000 to be used in a current research project. The equipment has alternative future use in other research projects after the current project ends. Under ASC 730-10-25, how should Whitfield account for the equipment?', '["Expense the $275,000 immediately as research and development cost", "Capitalize the $275,000 and depreciate it over its useful life", "Expense $275,000 over the life of the current research project only", "Capitalize $275,000 but recognize full impairment when the current project concludes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 730-10-25, tangible assets purchased for R&D that have alternative future use in other R&D projects or otherwise are capitalized and depreciated over their useful lives, with depreciation allocated to R&D expense during research periods. Choice A is incorrect because immediate expensing applies only to equipment with no alternative future use — Whitfield''s equipment qualifies for capitalization because it can be redeployed.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Impairment loss allocation within asset group with fair value floor
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Summit Corp. identifies that an asset group consisting of a building (carrying amount $800,000, fair value $700,000) and specialized equipment (carrying amount $400,000, fair value $250,000) is impaired. The group''s total fair value is $950,000 and the total impairment loss is $250,000. How should the impairment loss be allocated under ASC 360?', '["$100,000 to the building and $150,000 to equipment, because the building cannot be written below its $700,000 fair value", "$166,667 to the building and $83,333 to equipment based strictly on relative carrying amounts", "The entire $250,000 to equipment because it has the larger fair value deficit", "$125,000 to each asset, allocated equally regardless of relative carrying amounts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 360-10-35-28, impairment loss on an asset group is allocated pro rata by carrying amount; however, no asset may be written below individual fair value. The building''s pro rata share ($166,667) would breach its $700,000 floor, so only $100,000 is allocated to the building and the remaining $150,000 goes to equipment. Choice B is incorrect because it ignores the fair value floor, which would improperly reduce the building below fair value.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Effect of switching to accelerated method on financial ratios
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Redstone Corp. switches from straight-line to double-declining balance depreciation for its fleet of delivery trucks at the start of Year 1. The trucks were purchased in the prior year and are relatively new. What is the most likely effect on Redstone''s return on assets and net income in Year 1 compared to continuing straight-line?', '["Return on assets increases; net income increases", "Return on assets increases; net income decreases", "Return on assets decreases; net income decreases", "Return on assets is unchanged; net income decreases"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, DDB applies a higher rate in early years than straight-line, increasing depreciation expense and reducing net income. The higher accumulated depreciation also lowers total assets. Since the income reduction is proportionally larger, return on assets decreases along with net income. Although Choice B correctly identifies lower net income, it incorrectly concludes ROA increases — the numerator decline dominates the denominator effect for newer assets.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Component replacement with old component derecognition
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Garrison Manufacturing replaces the engine in a stamping press at a cost of $95,000. The original engine had a net book value of $18,000 at the time of replacement. The new engine extends the press''s useful life by 5 years. Under the component approach, what amount should Garrison capitalize for the new engine and what is the income statement effect?', '["Capitalize $77,000 and recognize no gain or loss", "Capitalize $95,000 and recognize no gain or loss", "Expense the entire $95,000 as a repair cost", "Capitalize $95,000 and recognize an $18,000 loss on disposal of the old engine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, when a major component is replaced, the old component''s net book value ($18,000) is derecognized as a loss on disposal. The new engine is capitalized at its full cost of $95,000 because it extends the asset''s useful life. Choice A ($77,000) is incorrect because it nets the old book value against the new cost; however, derecognition and capitalization are separate accounting events that must be recorded independently.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Interest capitalization ceases for independently usable parts
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Crestview Hotels begins constructing a resort on March 1, 2025. By September 30, the main building is substantially complete and ready for occupancy, but the adjacent pool facility remains under construction until December 1. The main building operates independently of the pool. What is the most likely effect on the interest capitalization period for each component?', '["Interest capitalization continues for both components until December 1 because the resort is one project", "Capitalization stops for the main building on September 30 and continues for the pool until December 1", "Capitalization stops for both on September 30 because the revenue-generating asset is complete", "Capitalization stops for the main building on March 1, 2026, when the full resort opens"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 835-20-25-5, when an asset has parts that can function independently, interest capitalization ceases for each part when that part is substantially complete and ready for its intended use. The main building is ready on September 30, while the pool continues until December 1. Choice A is incorrect because it treats the resort as a single unit rather than recognizing independently usable components as required by the standard.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Nonmonetary exchange lacking commercial substance with boot received (proportional gain)
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Harmon Transport exchanges a delivery van (cost $55,000, accumulated depreciation $30,000, fair value $32,000) for a similar delivery van from another carrier. Harmon also receives $5,000 cash. The exchange lacks commercial substance. How does the boot received most likely affect Harmon''s gain recognition?', '["Harmon recognizes a proportional gain of $1,094 because boot received is less than 25% of the exchange value", "Harmon recognizes the full $7,000 gain because receiving cash makes the exchange monetary", "Harmon recognizes no gain because the exchange lacks commercial substance", "Harmon recognizes a $5,000 gain equal to the boot received"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 845-10-30, when an exchange lacks commercial substance and boot received is less than 25% of fair value, the recipient recognizes a proportional gain. Boot of $5,000 / $32,000 = 15.6% (< 25%), so Harmon recognizes $7,000 × ($5,000 / $32,000) = $1,094. Although Choice C seems logical, the entity receiving boot must recognize a proportional gain on the monetary portion even when commercial substance is absent.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Partial-year DDB depreciation computation
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'On July 1, 2025, Easton Industries purchases equipment for $240,000 with a 4-year useful life and $40,000 salvage value. Easton uses double-declining balance with a half-year convention for mid-year acquisitions. What is the depreciation expense for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["$50,000", "$120,000", "$60,000", "$25,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, the DDB rate is 2 / useful life = 50%, applied to the full book value without subtracting salvage. For a half-year: $240,000 × 50% × 6/12 = $60,000. Choice A ($50,000) is incorrect because it subtracts salvage before applying the rate rather than using the unadjusted book value — DDB only considers salvage as a floor the book value cannot breach.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Held-for-sale subsequent fair value recovery
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'On October 1, 2025, Stonebridge Corp. reclassifies equipment to held for sale and recognizes an impairment loss of $60,000, writing the asset down to $340,000. By December 31, the fair value less costs to sell has increased to $380,000. How does this subsequent increase most likely affect Stonebridge''s financial statements?', '["No adjustment because impairment losses on held-for-sale assets cannot be reversed", "A $60,000 gain restoring the asset to its original $400,000 carrying amount", "A $40,000 gain with depreciation resuming on the $380,000 carrying amount", "A $40,000 gain increasing the carrying amount to $380,000 with no depreciation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 360-10-35-43, held-for-sale assets may be written up for subsequent fair value increases, but only up to the cumulative impairment previously recognized. The $40,000 increase ($380,000 − $340,000) does not exceed the $60,000 cumulative loss. Choice A is incorrect because it confuses this rule with held-and-used assets, whereas held-for-sale assets permit limited recovery and are not depreciated.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L3 — Exchange with commercial substance involving loss and boot paid
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Morningstar Logistics exchanges a specialized sorting machine (cost $180,000, accumulated depreciation $95,000, fair value $60,000) plus $25,000 cash for a newer sorting machine with a fair value of $85,000. The exchange has commercial substance. At what amount should Morningstar record the new machine, and what is the financial statement impact?', '["Record at $110,000 and recognize no gain or loss", "Record at $85,000 and recognize a $25,000 loss", "Record at $85,000 and recognize no gain or loss", "Record at $60,000 and recognize a $25,000 loss"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 845-10-30, nonmonetary exchanges with commercial substance are recorded at the fair value of the asset received ($85,000). The $25,000 loss equals carrying amount ($85,000) minus fair value ($60,000) of the old machine. Choice A ($110,000) is incorrect because using the carrying amount plus cash paid applies only to exchanges lacking commercial substance rather than fair-value-based exchanges with commercial substance.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Interest income on temporarily invested construction funds cannot offset capitalizable interest
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Holbrook Development begins constructing a distribution center with $4,000,000 in borrowings at 7%. During construction, Holbrook temporarily invests $1,200,000 of the borrowed funds and earns $42,000 in interest income. The CFO proposes offsetting the interest income against capitalizable interest to reduce the capitalized amount. How should the $42,000 interest income be treated under ASC 835-20?', '["Recognized as revenue separately; it cannot offset capitalizable interest", "Offset against capitalizable interest to avoid double-counting the borrowed funds", "Deferred and amortized over the constructed asset''s useful life", "Credited directly to the asset''s cost, reducing the total capitalized amount"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 835-20-15, interest revenue on temporarily invested construction funds is recognized as income in the period earned and cannot be offset against capitalizable interest. The two amounts are treated independently. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 835-20-15 explicitly prohibits this offset, unlike some international standards — the interest income and the capitalizable borrowing cost serve different economic purposes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — When depreciation begins
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Under U.S. GAAP, when does depreciation of a fixed asset begin?', '["On the date the purchase order is signed", "On the date the entity begins using the asset in production", "When the asset is available and ready for its intended use", "On the first day of the fiscal year following acquisition"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, depreciation begins when the asset is available and ready for its intended use, even if the entity has not yet placed it into active service. Choice B is incorrect because actual use is not required — unlike a usage-triggered model, an asset sitting idle in a ready state still depreciates because its economic benefits are available to the entity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — ARO recognition trigger requires legal obligation
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Under ASC 410-20, which condition must exist for an entity to recognize an asset retirement obligation?', '["The entity has a moral or ethical obligation to restore the environment", "A legal obligation to retire, dismantle, or remediate a long-lived asset exists", "Management has voluntarily committed to a future cleanup plan", "The estimated retirement cost exceeds 10% of the asset''s carrying amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 410-20-15, an ARO is recognized only when a legal obligation — imposed by law, regulation, or contract — requires the entity to retire a long-lived asset. Voluntary plans and ethical commitments do not create an ARO. Choice A is incorrect because moral obligations lack the enforceability required by ASC 410-20, unlike legally binding obligations imposed by statute, regulation, or contract.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Pre-service testing costs capitalized
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Under U.S. GAAP, how should costs incurred to test a newly acquired machine before it is ready for its intended use be classified?', '["Expensed as research and development costs", "Expensed as period costs in the current year", "Deferred and amortized as a separate intangible asset", "Capitalized as part of the machine''s acquisition cost"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 360-10-30, all costs necessary to bring an asset to the condition and location required for its intended use are capitalized, including testing and trial-run costs before the asset is placed in service. Choice B (period expense) is incorrect because pre-service testing is directly attributable to preparing the asset rather than representing a separate period cost — it is an integral part of the acquisition cost.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Component depreciation vs single-unit financial impact evaluation
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Kensington Properties acquires a commercial building for $4,200,000. An engineering study identifies three components: structural frame ($2,500,000, 40-year life), HVAC system ($900,000, 15-year life), and roof ($800,000, 20-year life). All have zero salvage value. Alternatively, the building could be depreciated as a single unit over 40 years. Evaluate the most likely effect of component depreciation versus single-unit depreciation on Year 1 expense.', '["Component depreciation increases Year 1 expense by $57,500 because shorter-lived components depreciate faster", "Component depreciation decreases Year 1 expense because each component uses a lower individual rate", "Both methods produce the same expense because total cost and depreciable base are identical", "Component depreciation increases Year 1 expense by $42,000 because only the HVAC exceeds the single-unit rate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, component depreciation allocates cost to major components with different useful lives. The result is $62,500 (frame, $2.5M/40) + $60,000 (HVAC, $900K/15) + $40,000 (roof, $800K/20) = $162,500 versus $105,000 single-unit ($4.2M/40). Choice C is incorrect because although total cost is identical, different component lives produce a higher weighted-average annual expense rather than matching the single-unit rate.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Impairment reversal: held-and-used prohibition vs held-for-sale recovery
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Prescott Manufacturing wrote down a production line (held and used) by $300,000 two years ago. Market conditions have improved and the line''s fair value now exceeds its carrying amount by $200,000. Separately, Prescott wrote down warehouse equipment (held for sale) by $120,000 last quarter, and its fair value less costs to sell has since recovered by $80,000. Evaluate the proper treatment for each recovery.', '["Recognize a $200,000 gain on the production line and an $80,000 gain on the equipment", "Recognize no recovery on either asset because U.S. GAAP prohibits all impairment reversals", "Recognize no recovery on the production line but recognize an $80,000 gain on the equipment", "Recognize a $200,000 gain on the production line but no recovery on the equipment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 360-10-35, impairment losses on held-and-used assets cannot be reversed — the written-down amount becomes the new cost basis. However, ASC 360-10-35-43 permits held-for-sale assets to be written up for fair value recoveries, limited to the cumulative impairment previously recognized. The $80,000 recovery (≤ $120,000 cumulative loss) is recognized. Choice B is incorrect because it applies the held-and-used prohibition to all assets, unlike the distinct recovery provisions for held-for-sale assets.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — PP&E definition
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Which of the following items qualifies as property, plant, and equipment under U.S. GAAP?', '["A patent purchased for exclusive use in manufacturing", "A delivery truck used in daily operations", "Inventory held for sale to customers", "A 30-day certificate of deposit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 360-10, property, plant, and equipment are tangible long-lived assets used in operations and not held for sale. A delivery truck meets all three criteria. Choice A (patent) is incorrect because it is an intangible asset under ASC 350 rather than PP&E, even though it is used in manufacturing. Choices C and D are not tangible long-lived operating assets.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Basket purchase allocation using relative fair values
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Bridgewater Corp. purchases land, a building, and equipment together for $750,000. Independent appraisals value the land at $200,000, the building at $480,000, and the equipment at $120,000. At what amounts should Bridgewater record each asset?', '["Land $200,000; Building $480,000; Equipment $70,000", "Land $250,000; Building $250,000; Equipment $250,000", "Land $150,000; Building $450,000; Equipment $150,000", "Land $187,500; Building $450,000; Equipment $112,500"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 360-10-30, a basket (lump-sum) purchase is allocated based on relative fair values. Total appraised value is $800,000. Land: $200/$800 × $750,000 = $187,500. Building: $480/$800 × $750,000 = $450,000. Equipment: $120/$800 × $750,000 = $112,500. Choice A is incorrect because recording assets at full appraised values rather than proportional allocation forces an arbitrary residual to the remaining asset.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Derecognition criteria for PP&E
(2, 'Fixed Assets', 'Under U.S. GAAP, when should a fixed asset be derecognized and removed from the balance sheet?', '["Upon disposal, abandonment, or when no future economic benefit is expected", "When the asset becomes fully depreciated, regardless of continued use", "At the end of each reporting period if fair value falls below carrying amount", "When accumulated depreciation exceeds 75% of original cost"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 360-10-40, a fixed asset is derecognized when it is disposed of by sale, abandonment, or exchange, or when no future economic benefit is expected. Choice B is incorrect because a fully depreciated asset that remains in use continues on the balance sheet — unlike disposal, full depreciation alone does not trigger derecognition because the entity still controls the asset.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
